      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The Alutiiq Museum and Archaeological Repository in Mill Bay showcase indigenous heritage alongside Russian colonial history from the late 18th century.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/mill_bay_alaska",
      "category": "culture",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Mill Bay’s climate is subpolar oceanic (Köppen Cfc), with cool summers, mild winters, and average temperatures moderated by North Pacific currents.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        6
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/mill_bay_alaska",
      "category": "nature",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "The area receives about 78 inches of precipitation each year, mostly rain, with winter snowfall around 70 inches, creating a wet, foggy environment.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        27,
        28
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/mill_bay_alaska",
      "category": "nature",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "Glacial history carved U‑shaped valleys and fjord‑like inlets around Mill Bay, leaving steep rocky shores and gravel/sand deposits near stream outlets.",
